src/
├── main.rs          # Starts the app (entrypoint, setup routes)
├── api/             # Controllers / route handlers
│   ├── mod.rs
│   ├── users.rs
│   └── payments.rs
├── domain/          # Enterprise logic (your Models)
│   ├── mod.rs
│   ├── user.rs
│   └── order.rs
├── services/        # Application services (business workflows, use cases)
│   ├── mod.rs
│   ├── auth.rs
│   └── billing.rs
├── infra/           # Database, external APIs, persistence details
│   ├── mod.rs
│   ├── db.rs
│   └── redis.rs
└── views/           # JSON serializers, templates, DTOs
    ├── mod.rs
    ├── user_view.rs
    └── order_view.rs

2:

Hexagonal / Clean Architecture – similar separation but instead of calling it MVC, you organize around domain vs adapters. This is very popular in Rust because it makes dependencies one-directional (infra depends on domain, not the other way).

Feature-based modularization – Instead of layers (model/view/controller), split by features:

src/features/
├── users/
│   ├── api.rs
│   ├── model.rs
│   └── service.rs
├── payments/
│   ├── api.rs
│   ├── model.rs
│   └── service.rs


This can be easier if you expect consumers with drastically different views per feature.


